1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,640
In the world of engineering, there's an old saying: caching is king.

2
00:00:03,640 --> 00:00:07,260
And in the age of AI agents, that still holds true.

3
00:00:07,260 --> 00:00:10,660
Engineers at Anthropic recently gave us ClaudeCode.

4
00:00:10,660 --> 00:00:13,590
Best practices for prompt caching design.

5
00:00:13,590 --> 00:00:15,250
But before we dive into the specifics,

6
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:19,060
First, let's take one minute to understand what prompt caching actually is.

7
00:00:19,060 --> 00:00:20,900
Every time you send a message to a large language model,

8
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:25,810
the backend has to process all the text you send—your system prompt, the chat history, and the new message.

9
00:00:25,810 --> 00:00:31,810
This process is the main culprit behind latency and costs. Every time you send a message, the backend has to encode the entire text from scratch.

10
00:00:31,810 --> 00:00:32,810
So, here's the problem.

11
00:00:33,810 --> 00:00:35,150
Let's say you chat with it for 20 turns.

12
00:00:35,150 --> 00:00:36,630
So, in that 20th request, it still has to re-process the first 19 rounds from the very first word.

13
00:00:36,630 --> 00:00:39,350
The first 19 rounds are exactly the same as before.

14
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:42,730
But the model still has to re-process everything from the very first word.

15
00:00:42,730 --> 00:00:45,890
All that for 19 rounds of wasted effort.

16
00:00:45,890 --> 00:00:48,810
And that's exactly what prompt caching is for.

17
00:00:48,810 --> 00:00:49,590
So, how does it work in a nutshell?

18
00:00:49,590 --> 00:00:51,760
You specify a breakpoint in your request.

19
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:55,080
The system then takes the encoding from the very beginning of the prompt up to that breakpoint,

20
00:00:55,080 --> 00:00:56,060
and stores the result.

21
00:00:56,060 --> 00:00:57,760
So next time, if the prefix is exactly the same,

22
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:00,460
This skips the redundant computations. It sets a breakpoint in the request, so the next time the prefix is identical, it can be reused directly.

23
00:01:00,460 --> 00:01:01,830
Let me give you an example.

24
00:01:01,830 --> 00:01:05,410
Imagine you had to rewrite the table of contents and the first few chapters every single time you worked on a paper.

25
00:01:05,410 --> 00:01:06,810
and then add the new content.

26
00:01:06,810 --> 00:01:07,950
But with caching,

27
00:01:07,950 --> 00:01:10,730
It's like the overlapping part is just fast-forwarded.

28
00:01:10,730 --> 00:01:15,990
Only the new segment needs to be written, while the key parts that hit the cache are reused.

29
00:01:15,990 --> 00:01:17,730
The price drops to a tenth of the original.

30
00:01:17,730 --> 00:01:22,010
The initial write costs 1.25 times more—you're spending an extra 25%—but after that, you save 90% on every subsequent request.

31
00:01:22,010 --> 00:01:24,670
But after that, you save 90% every single time.

32
00:01:24,670 --> 00:01:26,430
By default, it's stored for 5 minutes.

33
00:01:26,430 --> 00:01:29,370
And it renews automatically if there's a request within those 5 minutes.

34
00:01:29,370 --> 00:01:30,860
And there's no extra charge for it.

35
00:01:30,860 --> 00:01:33,260
You can also opt for the one-hour paid version.

36
00:01:33,260 --> 00:01:34,810
But there is a catch.

37
00:01:34,810 --> 00:01:35,930
If the content is too short,

38
00:01:35,930 --> 00:01:37,030
so it won't be cached.

39
00:01:37,030 --> 00:01:40,090
Generally, you need at least 1,024 tokens.

40
00:01:40,090 --> 00:01:44,340
And the newer models require 4,096.

41
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:46,080
So, for short prompts, there's no caching to help you out.

42
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:47,560
So, for a long conversation with about 100,000 characters, let's look at a real-world example.

43
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:50,380
For a 100,000-token long conversation, with caching turned off,

44
00:01:50,380 --> 00:01:53,460
With Claude Sonnet, each round costs you 0.30 US dollars.

45
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:54,770
But with caching enabled,

46
00:01:54,770 --> 00:01:57,090
The first one costs 0.375.

47
00:01:57,090 --> 00:02:02,840
After that, each round is just 0.03, saving you about 90% on input costs for 10 rounds of conversation.

48
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:04,500
And it's not just about saving money.

49
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:08,860
The more content we don't have to recompute, the shorter the TTFT, and the lower the latency.

50
00:02:08,860 --> 00:02:10,930
The faster the first word appears,

51
00:02:10,930 --> 00:02:14,590
The following seven best practices all boil down to one thing: how to maximize prefix cache hits. Once you understand this, the next seven tips will be much easier to grasp.

52
00:02:14,590 --> 00:02:16,310
And they all boil down to one thing.

53
00:02:16,310 --> 00:02:20,320
How to maximize the chances of the prefix hitting the cache.

54
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:23,840
Internally at Anthropic, they treat the cache hit rate

55
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:25,520
as a key infrastructure-level metric.

56
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:28,700
Its importance is almost on par with server uptime.

57
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:30,060
If the hit rate drops,

58
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:31,600
and trigger on-call alerts.

59
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:34,450
Engineers have to handle it as an online incident.

60
00:02:34,450 --> 00:02:37,410
The original term used was 'partial-level incident declaration'.

61
00:02:37,410 --> 00:02:40,730
This means you have to go through the full incident response process.

62
00:02:40,730 --> 00:02:42,970
This is way more serious than a regular alert.

63
00:02:42,970 --> 00:02:44,310
But what's even more critical is this:

64
00:02:44,310 --> 00:02:47,040
And a high cache hit rate doesn't just save money.

65
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:49,120
it also directly impacts the user experience.

66
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:51,720
This allows Anthropic to offer its paying users

67
00:02:51,720 --> 00:02:53,519
more generous usage quotas.

68
00:02:53,519 --> 00:02:55,199
The higher the cache hit rate, the more you can use it for the same price. The higher the cache hit rate...

69
00:02:55,199 --> 00:02:58,160
The higher the hit rate, the more you can use it for the same price. You get more value out of your subscription.

70
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:06,120
It's not just a nice-to-have optimization; it's the fundamental requirement for the entire system to even function. So, for Claude Code, caching isn't just a nice little optimization.

71
00:03:06,120 --> 00:03:09,100
Without caching, there would be no Claude Code.

72
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:10,560
And why is that?

73
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:14,800
AI programming assistants like Claude Code are inherently designed for long conversations.

74
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:16,820
A single session can have dozens of turns.

75
00:03:16,820 --> 00:03:23,940
Each round, you have to resend the entire conversation history. This means it's recalculated from scratch every single time, causing latency and costs to explode.

76
00:03:23,940 --> 00:03:27,440
And the core principle behind caching is prefix matching.

77
00:03:27,440 --> 00:03:29,780
as long as the prefix of the next request is the same as the last one,

78
00:03:29,780 --> 00:03:31,720
it can reuse the previous computation.

79
00:03:31,720 --> 00:03:35,160
And the most important lesson of all stems from this very concept.

80
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:37,640
So, since the cache relies on prefix matching,

81
00:03:37,640 --> 00:03:41,500
So, the order of the elements in your prompt becomes absolutely critical.

82
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:44,800
Here's how Anthropic lays it out in their best practices.

83
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:48,120
First, you put the system prompt and the tool definitions.

84
00:03:48,120 --> 00:03:51,500
This part is fixed and shared across all interactions.

85
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:53,640


86
00:03:53,640 --> 00:03:55,180
These are shared within the same project.

87
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:58,180
The third layer holds the context for the current drawing.

88
00:03:58,180 --> 00:04:00,220
This is only valid for the current conversation.

89
00:04:00,220 --> 00:04:03,460
And finally, at the very end, you have the chat messages that grow with each round of conversation.

90
00:04:03,460 --> 00:04:06,900
Each turn, only the last single sentence is added.

91
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:10,180
So, the things that are less likely to change should be placed at the very beginning.

92
00:04:10,180 --> 00:04:12,210
It's just like tidying up a desk.

93
00:04:12,210 --> 00:04:15,010
Reference books you rarely touch go on the bottom.

94
00:04:15,010 --> 00:04:17,350
Put the materials you need for this week in the middle, so you don't have to rummage through your entire desk every single day.

95
00:04:17,350 --> 00:04:19,990
and the draft you're working on today goes right on top.

96
00:04:19,990 --> 00:04:24,120
This way, when you sit down each day, you don't have to rummage through the entire desk. It's just like organizing a desk.

97
00:04:24,120 --> 00:04:26,320
And here are a few common pitfalls you'll want to watch out for.

98
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:30,300
The first one is embedding the current time into the fixed system prompt. The problem is, the current time changes every second.

99
00:04:30,300 --> 00:04:33,340
The entire cache is just wasted.

100
00:04:33,340 --> 00:04:37,300
The second one is using an unordered container to store the tool definitions.

101
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:39,560
The order of the tools in each request is different.

102
00:04:39,560 --> 00:04:41,200
The prefixes won't match.

103
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:44,260
Third, the tool parameters are changed.

104
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:45,980
Even if you just change a single field,

105
00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:49,010
the entire prefix cache is invalidated.

106
00:04:49,010 --> 00:04:50,630
Miss one tiny detail,

107
00:04:50,630 --> 00:04:52,470
the entire caching chain is broken.

108
00:04:52,470 --> 00:04:54,650
So, what if the information gets outdated? For instance, things like the time or a file's status... what do you do when that information is actually stale?

109
00:04:54,650 --> 00:04:59,440
So, for things like the current time or file status, here's what Anthropic does.

110
00:04:59,440 --> 00:05:04,169
Tuck the update into the next message.

111
00:05:04,169 --> 00:05:05,309
So, you attach a system reminder to the next user message, and the system prompt itself stays completely untouched. But how exactly does that work?

112
00:05:05,309 --> 00:05:08,480
You just attach a system reminder to the next user message.

113
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:10,780
and sneak the new information in there.

114
00:05:10,780 --> 00:05:12,760
This way, the system prompt stays completely untouched.

115
00:05:12,760 --> 00:05:14,700
The cache remains completely intact.

116
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:17,980
This separation keeps the cache stable. The system prompt is the foundation, locked in place.

117
00:05:17,980 --> 00:05:20,939
and the messages can be changed freely.

118
00:05:20,939 --> 00:05:23,520
and the conversation history can be modified as you please.

119
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:26,260
With this separation, the cache becomes rock-solid.

120
00:05:26,260 --> 00:05:29,919
Now, this next point might seem a bit counter-intuitive to many.

121
00:05:29,919 --> 00:05:33,239
Sounds reasonable, right? You might be thinking, "That makes perfect sense." For simple questions, just switch to a smaller model to save some money.

122
00:05:33,239 --> 00:05:37,650
And for tough questions, you switch back to the large model. It all sounds perfectly logical, right?

123
00:05:37,650 --> 00:05:42,000
But here's the thing: the cache is tied to the specific model.

124
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:45,920
Switching models means all the cached data you've built up before is completely invalidated.

125
00:05:45,920 --> 00:05:47,360
Rebuilding from scratch costs more than just having the large language model answer the simple question directly.

126
00:05:47,360 --> 00:05:50,160
The cost of rebuilding is often higher than just having the large language model answer that simple question directly.

127
00:05:50,160 --> 00:05:51,720
is even higher. So...

128
00:05:51,720 --> 00:05:57,760
So, Claude Code's strategy is to use the same model for the main conversation from start to finish.

129
00:05:57,760 --> 00:06:00,640
So, what do you do when you need a smaller model to do the work?

130
00:06:00,640 --> 00:06:02,000
delegate subtasks.

131
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:05,100
These subtasks have their own independent context and cache.

132
00:06:05,100 --> 00:06:07,680
This prevents it from contaminating the main conversation's cache chain.

133
00:06:07,680 --> 00:06:11,980
So, the specific approach is to have the main model first create a task handover.

134
00:06:11,980 --> 00:06:13,800
Specify that the context should be condensed.

135
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:16,200
and then passes it to a sub-task for execution.

136
00:06:16,200 --> 00:06:19,760
Once it's done, it only sends the result back to the main conversation.

137
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:22,960
And that's exactly how the exploration mode in ClaudeCode works.

138
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:27,340
It uses a smaller model to perform exploration tasks within an isolated context.

139
00:06:27,340 --> 00:06:28,870
Let me give you an analogy.

140
00:06:28,870 --> 00:06:33,430
You wouldn't have an intern sit at your desk and use your computer. You wouldn't, just to make things easier,

141
00:06:33,430 --> 00:06:35,790
Instead, you'd assign them their own separate machine.

142
00:06:35,790 --> 00:06:38,039
Write out the task instructions clearly and send them over.

143
00:06:38,039 --> 00:06:40,279
and have them send the results back when they're done.

144
00:06:40,279 --> 00:06:42,980
And here's a word of warning for those of you thinking about using intermediaries.

145
00:06:42,980 --> 00:06:45,580
The cache is isolated by account.

146
00:06:45,580 --> 00:06:48,350
I've seen some people trying to use an account pool for relaying requests.

147
00:06:48,350 --> 00:06:49,650
Once you start mixing up your account pool,

148
00:06:49,650 --> 00:06:50,690
If the hit rate is too low,

149
00:06:50,690 --> 00:06:53,170
You don't make any money, and on top of that, you get your account banned.

150
00:06:53,170 --> 00:06:56,360
Don't just switch back and forth after a couple of messages. And be wary of anyone who tells you to just keep swapping accounts like it's nothing.

151
00:06:56,360 --> 00:07:00,230
And the same goes for constantly switching to the next prompt after just a couple of messages.

152
00:07:00,230 --> 00:07:03,100
Don't change the tool set during a conversation.

153
00:07:03,100 --> 00:07:07,700
Intuitively, you might think the current task only needs three tools.

154
00:07:07,700 --> 00:07:11,100
Wouldn't it be cleaner to just get rid of the other 30?

155
00:07:11,100 --> 00:07:14,060
Adding or removing even one tool breaks the cache. That's because the tool definitions are part of the prompt prefix.

156
00:07:14,060 --> 00:07:19,000
So, add one or remove one, and the entire cache chain is broken.

157
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:22,040
This means the entire conversation cache has to be rebuilt from scratch.

158
00:07:22,040 --> 00:07:26,600
The cost is far greater than the tiny bit of space you save by not including a few extra tool definitions.

159
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:27,900
It might seem like an optimization,

160
00:07:27,900 --> 00:07:29,300
But it just ends up making a mess.

161
00:07:29,300 --> 00:07:32,039
ClaudeCode has a Plan Mode.

162
00:07:32,039 --> 00:07:34,459
Once entered, the model only thinks.

163
00:07:34,459 --> 00:07:37,020
It only plans, it doesn't execute.

164
00:07:37,020 --> 00:07:41,720
Now, intuitively, you'd think that when you enter Plan Mode, you'd remove all the execution tools. But that's not what Anthropic did.

165
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:44,020
remove them and then add them back in.

166
00:07:44,020 --> 00:07:46,920
But that's not what Anthropic did.

167
00:07:46,920 --> 00:07:49,740
Instead, their approach was to keep all the tools in place.

168
00:07:49,740 --> 00:07:51,909
They also added two special tools.

169
00:07:51,909 --> 00:07:55,309
Entering and exiting the planning phase.

170
00:07:55,309 --> 00:07:58,370
When the model is invoked, it switches to 'thinking mode' once it enters 'planning'.

171
00:07:58,370 --> 00:08:00,650
and calling 'exit planning' brings it back.

172
00:08:00,650 --> 00:08:04,659
So, how do we communicate the constraint that nothing should be executed in Plan Mode?

173
00:08:04,659 --> 00:08:07,919
By inserting a system message into the conversation,

174
00:08:07,919 --> 00:08:10,639
This tells the model that it's currently in the planning phase.

175
00:08:10,639 --> 00:08:15,900
Now, keep in mind, inserting a message into the conversation flow isn't the same as changing the system prompt. You're just inserting a message into the conversation flow.

176
00:08:15,900 --> 00:08:18,000
It's crucial to distinguish between these two. Inserting a message into the conversation flow is not the same as changing the system prompt.

177
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,100
The system prompt is fixed and part of the cache prefix.

178
00:08:21,100 --> 00:08:22,580
while the conversation messages are dynamic.

179
00:08:22,580 --> 00:08:24,090
This doesn't affect the prefix.

180
00:08:24,090 --> 00:08:26,110
This way, the set of tools remains constant.

181
00:08:26,110 --> 00:08:27,750
So the cache stays valid.

182
00:08:27,750 --> 00:08:30,669
For complex tasks, it figures out the plan before taking action—no manual switching needed from you. And on top of that, it brings an extra benefit.

183
00:08:30,669 --> 00:08:34,188
For complex tasks, it figures out the plan before taking action. You don't have to manually switch modes; the model can decide for itself when to enter 'Plan Mode'.

184
00:08:34,188 --> 00:08:35,708
When it encounters a complex task,

185
00:08:35,708 --> 00:08:38,149
It thinks things through before taking action.

186
00:08:38,149 --> 00:08:39,909
You don't have to switch modes manually.

187
00:08:39,909 --> 00:08:43,270
Claude Code might need to connect to dozens of external tools.

188
00:08:43,270 --> 00:08:46,810
So, both paths are a dead end. Sticking all the full definitions in there just takes up way too much space.

189
00:08:46,810 --> 00:08:49,630
But adding or removing them on the fly would just break the cache.

190
00:08:49,630 --> 00:08:53,410
The compromise solution Anthropic came up with is called lazy loading.

191
00:08:53,410 --> 00:08:57,950
Initially, you just provide a lightweight placeholder. All the model sees is the tool's name.

192
00:08:57,950 --> 00:09:00,969
without the full parameter definitions and all that.

193
00:09:00,969 --> 00:09:02,309
When the model actually needs to use a specific tool,

194
00:09:02,309 --> 00:09:05,969
Then, it uses a tool search function to pull in the complete definition.

195
00:09:05,969 --> 00:09:10,870
The advantage is that the prefix always contains only those lightweight placeholders.

196
00:09:10,870 --> 00:09:14,610
It won't change just because a tool gets loaded.

197
00:09:14,610 --> 00:09:18,000
This keeps the cache stable, acting just like a library's card catalog.

198
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,600
First, you consult the index to find the book you want,

199
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:21,880
and then go get it from the shelf.

200
00:09:21,880 --> 00:09:24,640
Without having to haul every single book to your desk.

201
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:25,890
And it's worth noting,

202
00:09:25,890 --> 00:09:29,590
This tool's search function is now publicly available through an API.

203
00:09:29,590 --> 00:09:33,500
This allows developers to use it directly, simplifying their own tool management.

204
00:09:33,500 --> 00:09:36,840
And the last one is about context compression.

205
00:09:36,840 --> 00:09:41,430
This is also the most technically clever one. As a long conversation runs on...

206
00:09:41,430 --> 00:09:43,410
the context window will get filled up.

207
00:09:43,410 --> 00:09:46,790
At this point, you need to compress the previous conversation into a summary.

208
00:09:46,790 --> 00:09:48,670
to make room for the conversation to continue.

209
00:09:48,670 --> 00:09:50,110
But here's the problem.

210
00:09:50,110 --> 00:09:52,590
If you start a whole new request just for the compression,

211
00:09:52,590 --> 00:09:54,490
and used a different system prompt,

212
00:09:54,490 --> 00:09:56,100
or didn't include the tool definitions,

213
00:09:56,100 --> 00:10:00,580
So, right from the very first word, it won't match the cache from the main conversation.

214
00:10:00,580 --> 00:10:03,910
The two cache chains are completely independent of each other.

215
00:10:03,910 --> 00:10:07,310
The longer the conversation, the more expensive the API call. Plus, you have to send the entire conversation over just to get a summary.

216
00:10:07,310 --> 00:10:09,230
The longer the conversation, the more expensive the call. And at this point, you're paying for the full, un-cached request.

217
00:10:09,230 --> 00:10:11,130
the cost without any cache discount.

218
00:10:11,130 --> 00:10:13,069
The longer the conversation, the more expensive it gets. And the longer the conversation, the more expensive this call becomes.

219
00:10:13,069 --> 00:10:17,369
Anthropic's solution is called Cache-Safe Forking.

220
00:10:17,369 --> 00:10:21,369
For the compression request, you must use the exact same system prompt as the main conversation.

221
00:10:21,369 --> 00:10:23,769
user context, tool definitions,

222
00:10:23,769 --> 00:10:26,660
Include the messages from the main conversation as the history.

223
00:10:26,660 --> 00:10:29,240
and then append a compression instruction at the end.

224
00:10:29,240 --> 00:10:30,880
as a new user message.

225
00:10:30,880 --> 00:10:32,500
From a backend perspective,

226
00:10:32,500 --> 00:10:35,319
This request is almost identical to the last one.

227
00:10:35,319 --> 00:10:36,659
The same prefix,

228
00:10:36,659 --> 00:10:37,919
the same tools,

229
00:10:37,919 --> 00:10:39,470
And the same history.

230
00:10:39,470 --> 00:10:42,950
So, the prefix cache can be reused directly.

231
00:10:42,950 --> 00:10:47,220
This request is almost identical to the last one. The only new cost is the final compression instruction itself.

232
00:10:47,220 --> 00:10:50,000
Allocate a buffer in advance. You can't wait for the window to be full to start compressing; you also need to reserve a compression buffer space.

233
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,240
Leave some space for the summary output.

234
00:10:52,240 --> 00:10:54,740
You can't wait for the window to be full before you start compressing.

235
00:10:54,740 --> 00:10:57,010
You need to leave some buffer in advance.

236
00:10:57,010 --> 00:11:00,590
A single compression operation can reuse the entire cache from the main conversation history.

237
00:11:00,590 --> 00:11:02,210
at almost no extra cost.

238
00:11:02,210 --> 00:11:08,090
Looking back at these seven tips, it all boils down to one thing: caching is all about prefix matching.

239
00:11:08,090 --> 00:11:13,370
First, prefix matching is everything. Any change, anywhere in the prefix,

240
00:11:13,370 --> 00:11:16,170
will invalidate the cache for everything that comes after it.

241
00:11:16,170 --> 00:11:21,310
Second, use messages instead of system prompts. If you need to change the mode, do it by sending a message.

242
00:11:21,310 --> 00:11:22,650
Need to update the time?

243
00:11:22,650 --> 00:11:24,940
and stuff them into the conversation messages.

244
00:11:24,940 --> 00:11:26,740
Don't touch the system prompt.

245
00:11:26,740 --> 00:11:30,930
Third, don't switch tools or models mid-conversation.

246
00:11:30,930 --> 00:11:33,890
Use tools to represent state transitions.

247
00:11:33,890 --> 00:11:37,240
Instead of adding or removing tools, use lazy loading.

248
00:11:37,240 --> 00:11:39,880
Fourth, monitor it just like you would your system's uptime.

249
00:11:39,880 --> 00:11:41,589
Monitor the cache hit rate.

250
00:11:41,589 --> 00:11:43,989
Anthropic triggers an alert for any cache interruption.

251
00:11:43,989 --> 00:11:45,529
When dealing with online incidents,

252
00:11:45,529 --> 00:11:49,690
Fifth, any forked operations must share the prefix of the main conversation.

253
00:11:49,690 --> 00:11:54,160
Use the same parameters for all subtasks in the summary compression.

254
00:11:54,160 --> 00:11:57,580
Switching models, changing accounts, or starting from scratch—it's all the same principle. All design decisions must revolve around this one rule.

255
00:11:57,580 --> 00:11:58,760
Don't change the instructions.

256
00:11:58,760 --> 00:11:59,940
Don't touch the tools.

257
00:11:59,940 --> 00:12:02,960
Switching models, changing accounts, or starting from scratch—it's all the same. The moment you touch it, the entire cache chain breaks.

258
00:12:02,960 --> 00:12:05,000
Switching models, changing accounts...

259
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:06,180
or starting from scratch.

260
00:12:06,180 --> 00:12:07,970
Same principle.

261
00:12:08,450 --> 00:12:10,210
This looks like cache optimization.

262
00:12:10,210 --> 00:12:12,380
Embrace the constraint, then build the system around it. But it's still a valid way of thinking.

263
00:12:12,380 --> 00:12:14,580
First, you have to recognize that unavoidable constraint.

264
00:12:14,580 --> 00:12:17,580
And then, build the entire system around it.

